Yes. After re-checking the current specs and benchmark sources, I would tighten the previous answer substantially.

The **canonical resource should not prescribe one framework**. It should prescribe an architecture and measurable contracts, then let an agent choose the fastest implementation for each workload.

As of **August 21, 2026**, the important current standards are MCP **2026-07-28**, A2A **1.0.0**, and OpenAPI **3.2.0**. MCP's latest release is especially important because it changed the optimal agent architecture: it is now stateless at the protocol layer, self-describing per request, cacheable, routable using HTTP headers, and designed to work behind ordinary global HTTP infrastructure. ([Model Context Protocol Blog][1])

# Canonical Agent-First Performance Architecture — 2026

I would make this the standard for essentially every utility/product you build.

```text
                         INTERNET
                            │
               ┌────────────┴────────────┐
               │                         │
             HUMAN                     AGENT
               │                         │
               ▼                ┌────────┼─────────┐
        semantic website        │        │         │
                                ▼        ▼         ▼
                              MCP      REST      A2A*
                                │        │
                                └────┬───┘
                                     │
                              PRODUCT CORE
                                     │
                     ┌───────────────┼──────────────┐
                     │               │              │
               deterministic      data layer       AI
                   engine                           │
                     │                  ┌────────────┼────────────┐
                     │                  │            │            │
                  Rust/TS          browser AI    edge AI     GPU AI
                                                   
*A2A only if the product itself exposes an autonomous agent
```

The central rule is:

> **One product core, multiple interfaces.**

The website, REST API and MCP server should **not contain separate business logic**.

For example:

```text
domains.search()
      │
      ├── website
      ├── POST /v1/domains/search
      └── MCP search_domains
```

All three invoke the same function/library.

That is both faster and much easier for coding agents to maintain.

---

# 1. Optimize differently for humans and agents

This is the most important refinement.

For a human, performance means things like:

* time to visible content
* interaction latency
* animation/render responsiveness
* time to first useful result

For an agent, the bigger performance costs are often:

* **number of reasoning turns**
* number of tool calls
* schema discovery cost
* tokens consumed by tool descriptions
* tokens returned by tools
* network round trips
* ambiguous outputs requiring another model turn
* failure/retry rate

So agent performance is not:

> JSON parsing is 0.4 ms faster.

It is often:

> The agent solved the entire problem in one tool invocation rather than six.

That can save **seconds**, inference tokens and money.

I would therefore track an additional family of metrics:

```text
Agent completion latency
Tool calls / completed task
Model turns / completed task
Input tokens / completed task
Tool-result tokens / completed task
Schema tokens exposed
Retry rate
Invalid tool-call rate
Useful results / returned token
```

Those should become first-class performance metrics.

---

# 2. MCP should be the preferred reasoning interface

You were right to emphasize MCP.

For an agent choosing how to use one of your products, I would expose:

```text
1. MCP
2. REST/OpenAPI
3. Website
```

but each serves a different purpose.

### MCP

Best for:

* tool discovery
* agent reasoning
* normal agent operations
* structured task execution

### REST

Best for:

* bulk operations
* scripts
* integrations
* very high throughput
* data export
* streaming large result sets

### Website

Best for:

* humans
* browser agents
* discovery
* documentation
* fallback when MCP isn't connected

The latest MCP release actually makes this significantly better than it was six months ago.

MCP `2026-07-28` removed the required initialization handshake and protocol-level session. Every request can be independently routed to any server instance. It also added `Mcp-Method` and `Mcp-Name` headers explicitly so gateways can route, meter and authorize requests without parsing the JSON body. ([Model Context Protocol Blog][1])

That is excellent architecture for Cloudflare/global edge deployments.

---

# 3. Do NOT make MCP a collection of 40 tiny tools

This matters enormously.

Bad MCP design for the domain product:

```text
generate_words
generate_synonyms
generate_domains
normalize_domains
score_domains
check_domain
check_domains
rank_domain
rank_domains
lookup_price
...
```

The model now has to orchestrate your internal architecture.

That creates reasoning latency.

Instead, expose **task-level tools**.

For the domain product I'd probably expose only:

```text
search_domains
check_domains
domain_details
```

Potentially:

```text
continue_domain_search
```

That's it.

The main tool:

```text
search_domains
```

does:

```text
concept expansion
      ↓
candidate generation
      ↓
deduplication
      ↓
ranking
      ↓
batch availability
      ↓
final reranking
      ↓
results
```

inside the service.

An agent can therefore say:

```text
Find 30 available .com domains for an
AI agent news website resembling
AgentWatch and AgentSpy.
Search at least 2000 candidates.
```

One call.

That's the correct agent abstraction.

---

# 4. MCP schemas should be extremely strong

Current MCP tools support full JSON Schema 2020-12 in the `2026-07-28` specification, including composition and conditionals; output schemas are no longer constrained to simple objects, and structured content can be arbitrary JSON. ([Model Context Protocol Blog][2])

Use that.

Don't define:

```json
{
  "query": "string"
}
```

when you can define the actual contract.

For example:

```json
{
  "description": "AI agent ecosystem news site",
  "liked_names": [
    "AgentWatch",
    "AgentSpy"
  ],
  "tlds": ["com"],
  "search_depth": 1000,
  "constraints": {
    "max_length": 14,
    "allow_hyphens": false,
    "allow_numbers": false
  },
  "result_limit": 20
}
```

And respond with something like:

```json
{
  "search_id": "ds_...",
  "searched": 1000,
  "checked": 742,
  "available_count": 31,
  "results": [
    {
      "domain": "example.com",
      "score": 0.94,
      "availability": "confirmed",
      "provider": "namesilo"
    }
  ],
  "next_cursor": "..."
}
```

No prose parsing required.

---

# 5. Minimize MCP result tokens

This is another place where an agent-first app differs radically from a human application.

Imagine your search finds:

```text
138 available domains
```

Don't dump 138 richly described objects into the context window.

Return:

```text
top 20
+
summary
+
search_id
+
cursor
```

Then make the full data available through:

```text
resource
API
pagination
export URL
```

The model probably only needs the best few.

This principle is:

> **Compute richly; communicate sparsely.**

It reduces:

* inference latency
* context consumption
* cost
* distraction
* tool-response parsing

while retaining access to the underlying data.

