# 9. REST remains extremely important

I would never make a product MCP-only.

Expose:

```text
/v1/...
```

with a proper **OpenAPI 3.2** definition.

OpenAPI 3.2 is currently the latest published specification and explicitly exists so humans and machines can discover and understand HTTP APIs without reading source code or reverse-engineering network traffic. ([OpenAPI Initiative Publications][5])

Your canonical API structure could be:

```text
/openapi.json

/v1/domains/search
/v1/domains/check
/v1/domains/{domain}
```

The API should be:

```text
JSON
UTF-8
stable
versioned
strictly typed
cursor-paginated
batch-aware
cache-aware
idempotent where appropriate
```

---

# 10. Agent APIs should be high-level AND bulk-capable

Traditional CRUD APIs often look like:

```text
GET /domain/foo.com
GET /domain/bar.com
GET /domain/baz.com
```

Terrible for agents.

Instead support:

```text
POST /v1/domains/check
```

with:

```json
{
  "domains": [
    "foo.com",
    "bar.com",
    "baz.com"
  ]
}
```

One network request.

This applies everywhere.

If an agent might reasonably want 100 things, your API should probably accept 100 things.

Batching wins far more performance than switching Node → Rust in many APIs.

---

# 11. Streaming: direct API should be excellent at it

For operations producing progressively useful output:

```text
SSE
```

or

```text
NDJSON
```

are excellent external interfaces.

Example:

```text
POST /v1/domains/search
Accept: application/x-ndjson
```

Response:

```json
{"type":"started","candidates":1000}
{"type":"progress","checked":200}
{"type":"domain","domain":"foo.com","score":0.92}
{"type":"domain","domain":"bar.com","score":0.89}
{"type":"complete","checked":1000}
```

Agents can start reasoning on early results.

Humans can render them immediately.

Don't artificially wait to construct one enormous final object.

---

# 12. HTTP/3 matters, but don't build it yourself

At the internet edge, HTTP/3/QUIC gives independent streams, so packet loss on one request stream doesn't block progress on others as it can at the TCP transport level. ([RFC Editor][6])

But this should be:

> infrastructure configuration

rather than:

> application code.

Put Cloudflare or an equivalent CDN/network in front and let it negotiate the best transport.

---

# 13. Use HTTP caching properly

Your API needs:

```text
Cache-Control
ETag
If-None-Match
stale-while-revalidate
stale-if-error
```

where semantics permit.

Cloudflare's current Workers caching layer supports those normal HTTP concepts and can even serve stale content on backend failures. ([Cloudflare Docs][7])

For domain checking:

```text
TAKEN
TTL = hours

AVAILABLE
TTL = minutes

UNKNOWN
TTL = seconds
```

For blog posts:

```text
immutable/versioned assets
TTL = effectively forever
```

For API documentation:

```text
long cache + ETag
```

