# 14. Website architecture: static HTML by default

I would correct my earlier “Astro + Solid” statement slightly.

The hierarchy should actually be:

```text
HTML/CSS
    ↓
vanilla JS if sufficient
    ↓
small reactive island if necessary
    ↓
full application framework only if justified
```

The current JS Framework Benchmark still shows the fastest implementations clustering around highly optimized vanilla/minimal implementations; its latest official results are Chrome 150 from July 2026. ([Krausest][8])

So the canonical rule should be:

> **Ship the least JavaScript capable of implementing the interaction.**

Not:

> always use Solid.

Solid is a reasonable high-performance default when a reactive UI is genuinely useful.

But a domain finder might need surprisingly little framework code.

---

# 15. Static assets should bypass compute

For Cloudflare:

```text
/
styles.css
app.js
/blog/...
```

should normally be served directly as static assets.

Only:

```text
/api/*
/mcp
```

should invoke Worker code.

Cloudflare Static Assets automatically cache static files across the network. ([Cloudflare Docs][9])

Critically, don't configure:

```text
run Worker first for everything
```

unless required.

Cloudflare itself notes that doing so can add latency, particularly when Smart Placement puts Worker execution near a backend instead of the user. ([Cloudflare Docs][10])

Ideal:

```text
assets → nearest edge

API → optimally placed compute
```

---

# 16. Site performance targets should be stricter than Google's minimums

Google's current “good” Core Web Vital thresholds remain:

```text
LCP ≤ 2.5 s
INP ≤ 200 ms
CLS ≤ 0.1
```

at the 75th percentile. ([web.dev][11])

Those should be considered **minimum acceptable performance**, not your goal.

For your lightweight utility products I'd aim for:

```text
p75 LCP             < 1.0–1.5 s
p75 INP             < 100 ms
CLS                  < 0.05

cached HTML TTFB     < 50 ms
initial HTML gzip    < 30–50 KB
initial JS gzip      < 50 KB preferred
initial CSS gzip     < 20 KB preferred
```

Not every sophisticated app will hit those bundle numbers, but every increase should have to justify itself.

---

# 17. Keep work off the browser main thread

Expensive:

```text
name generation
embeddings
phonetic ranking
large JSON parsing
local inference
compression
```

can go into:

```text
Web Worker
```

or WebGPU/WASM as appropriate.

Web.dev specifically recommends moving CPU-heavy work off the main thread to improve responsiveness/INP. ([web.dev][12])

For your domain app:

```text
UI thread:
inputs + DOM updates

Worker:
generate 100k candidates
score
cluster
dedupe
```

The interface remains instant.

---

# 18. Rust still has an important role

TechEmpower Round 23 remains the current published server framework round, and Rust implementations occupy many of the highest positions; Axum's PostgreSQL implementation, for example, was above one million responses/sec in the Fortunes workload. ([TechEmpower][13])

That supports:

> Rust is an excellent native high-performance server language.

It does **not** imply:

> Rust should replace JavaScript everywhere.

Canonical allocation:

```text
Browser glue                 TypeScript
Edge HTTP orchestration      TypeScript
CPU-intensive browser code   Rust → WASM if worthwhile
CPU-intensive server code    Rust
very high-performance API    Rust/Axum
simple I/O API               TS Worker or Go/Rust
GPU inference                Python/C++ serving stack
```

---

# 19. Why TypeScript still wins at the edge

Cloudflare Workers' platform APIs are natively exposed through the web/JavaScript runtime.

Rust Workers compile through WebAssembly, and Cloudflare currently notes:

* Wasm bundles tend to be larger than equivalent JavaScript
* larger bundles can increase startup latency
* Workers Wasm doesn't support threading ([Cloudflare Docs][14])

So this:

```text
TS edge orchestrator
      ↓
Rust native service
```

can outperform a doctrinaire:

```text
Rust everywhere
```

in practical end-to-end architecture.

---

# 20. Placement beats language surprisingly often

If your Worker calls a database or external API repeatedly, put computation near the backend.

Cloudflare's current placement system can:

```text
automatically choose placement
target AWS region
target GCP region
target hostname
target TCP host
```

and Cloudflare says this can produce single-digit-millisecond connections to traditional cloud backends. ([Cloudflare Docs][15])

So:

```text
User Cambodia
↓
edge asset Cambodia

API request
↓
Cloudflare network
↓
Worker near database Singapore
↓
database ~2 ms
```

may be substantially faster than:

```text
Worker Cambodia
↓
database Frankfurt
↓
database Frankfurt
↓
database Frankfurt
```

even though the first Worker is geographically farther from the user.

