p95 latency
p99 latency
throughput
error rate
cache-hit rate
payload bytes

agents
------
tool calls/task
model turns/task
tool schema tokens
result tokens/task
task completion latency
invalid invocation rate

AI
--
TTFT
TPOT
ITL
tokens/sec
GPU utilization
cost/request

system
------
CPU
memory
cold start
network RTT
DB RTT
external dependency latency
```

And use the actual workload, not synthetic framework reputation.

The benchmark references underneath that canonical resource should be:

* **TechEmpower Round 23** for server/framework baselines. ([TechEmpower][13])
* **js-framework-benchmark Chrome 150** for DOM/framework comparisons. ([Krausest][8])
* **Speedometer 3.1** for browser application responsiveness. ([BrowserBench][25])
* **Core Web Vitals** for real-user browser performance. ([web.dev][26])
* **SGLang/vLLM/TensorRT benchmark harnesses** for AI-serving decisions. ([GitHub][17])
* **MCP 2026-07-28** for agent tool architecture. ([Model Context Protocol Blog][1])
* **OpenAPI 3.2** for machine-readable HTTP APIs. ([OpenAPI Initiative Publications][5])
* **A2A 1.0** only when the service is itself an agent. ([A2A Protocol][24])

So the compact doctrine I'd put at the top of every project is:

> **Static-first. Semantic-first. Local-first. Deterministic-first. Cache-first. Batch-first. Parallel-first. Stream-first. Edge for orchestration. Rust for measured compute hot paths. AI outside the critical path whenever possible. REST for universal high-throughput machine access. MCP for low-turn, strongly typed agent operation. A2A only for autonomous-agent interoperability. One product core behind every interface. Optimize agent turns and tokens as aggressively as human milliseconds. Benchmark the actual workload and let measured performance—not framework fashion—choose the implementation.**

That is considerably closer to a genuinely canonical **agent-era high-performance web architecture** than my earlier “use Astro/Solid/Rust” answer.

